  "summary": "Traditional Austrian establishments serving coffee and pastries in elegant, contemplative settings.",
  "description": "Viennese coffeehouse interiors feature marble tables, Thonet chairs, leather stools, and extensive newspapers. This aesthetic developed from 17th-century coffee houses, reflecting Vienna's coffee culture as centers of intellectual life, literature, and leisure for the bourgeoisie.",
  "culturalContext": "The Viennese coffeehouse (Wiener Kaffeehaus, UNESCO intangible heritage 2011) — café central of Freud, Trotsky and Zweig's essays — built intellectual Europe's living room: Thonet bentwood chairs, marble-top tables, newspaper holders (Zeitungsstock) with polished wooden rods, crystal chandeliers over smoky mirrors, and the unwritten right to stay for hours over one Melange. Café Central, Sperl, Landtmann survive; the aesthetic exports as \"old world coffeehouse\" universal.",
